It's really the best first step for you
in your professional development, laying

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.960
that foundation to develop strong
communication skills. Moving forward I

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.510
encourage you to watch all seven lessons.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.720
There's a little practice involved in
each one so you can actually hear and

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:32.500
then try the ideas out. Let's jump right
into the first lesson.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.930
In lesson one we're going after long-windedness.
I encourage you to watch this and go

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.730
through as many times as you need to to
make this material a habit. Tip one. Long-

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.590
windedness is a key enemy of good
communication. I'm always surprised that

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.430
really talkative people assume that
they're good communicators. But, more

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.379
talking does not equal good
communication. In fact, a lot of times if

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:02.520
these words--gabby, chatty, verbose, and
talkative--accurately describe you

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.570
it's probably forming a bad impression.
Especially in professional settings

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.820
Alison Green said it like this. You might
think well some people long winded but

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.979
it doesn't mean he wouldn't do a good
job.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:17.490
The problem is that a minimum is signals
that you're not good at picking up on

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.659
conversational cues and it raises doubts
about your ability to organize your

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:27.509
thoughts and can convey needed information
quickly. So, strategy one, resolve why you

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:32.549
sometimes talk too much. When do you find yourself multiplying words being overly

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:37.530
talkative? Is it just at work or is it
social situations? And, what are your in-

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.680
the-moment thought processes that
trigger it?

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.149
Are you insecure about something? What's going on in your mind that's driving

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:50.880
that over talkative habit? Because all
the tips in the world will not undo an

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.500
unspoken belief that drives that
long-windedness. So, excessive

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.619
talkativeness is rooted in our unspoken
beliefs

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.240
let's say you're a physician and you're
thinking to yourself, maybe you don't

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.380
realize it, but you're thinking I want
people to know I'm smart. You will most

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.819
certainly talk too much to prove that.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:14.349
And because we live in a polite society,
people just play along. But inside

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:19.030
they're having an entirely different
thought process about this conversation.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.189
They will almost never tell you directly that you're long-winded however they will form

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:29.379
unfavorable impressions of you anyway.
And so it's very important to realize

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:33.250
the impact that long-winded
communication can have on your

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.849
professional relationships and the
people around you. So people are long- winded

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:40.359
for various reasons. What's your reason?
Maybe there's more than one. Is it

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:46.090
insecurity? Maybe some self-centeredness?
Do you want to show everything you know

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.319
like you're that physician? Do you like the
air time and attention? In other words,

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:54.069
does it feel good when people are
listening to you and you love when

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:59.500
everyone's watching you in action? Do you
mistake talking for adding value? Do

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.400
mistake more talking for good
communication? Are there some control

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.959
issues going on in your heart, in your
mind? In other words, do you like to

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:12.579
steer a conversation and be the one
talking so you dictate the pace and the

00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:16.840
flow of things. Any one of these reasons,
and there are many others, could be

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.919
driving that long-windedness. So, it's
really important that you take some time

00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:23.290
and get to the bottom of it.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:28.359
Strategy number two is to gauge your
long-windedness. Usually there are

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:32.949
places and times when you're not
necessarily long-winded and other times you are.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:44.350
Strategy two is to gauge your long-windedness. Rate yourself on a scale of 1 to

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:48.520
10. If you're really concise, give
yourself a 1. If you're somewhere in the middle,

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.470
depending on situation, 5. If you're
really long-winded, a 10. And, of course,

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.459
you can give yourself other scores. But I
would recommend setting a goal to move

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:02.140
at least below 5. Let's say you want
to be from a 1 to 4 range, depending upon

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:08.230
the situation. Concise strategy number
three is to commit to conciseness. As FDR

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:12.310
said be sincere, be brief, and be seated.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:16.840
He's talking about presentations, it
sounds like. But I think this demonstrates

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:23.410
his commitment to conciseness. Because
conciseness really sets the stage for

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:28.510
that clarity and that confidence that
we're all after. And so commitment in

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:34.450
this sense means practicing the tips in
this video in the next six lessons. And

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.530
while you're doing and remember why you
want to be concise, clear, and confident.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.610
Here are some reasons. People will pay
attention to what you say when you

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:47.740
communicate this way. People remember
what you say. You'll have more impact. And

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:52.780
you can lead more effectively. So there
are lots of good reasons to do so.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:58.419
So, long-winded this is a key enemy to good communication. Some take-away strategies.

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:03.580
Resolve why you sometimes talk too much. Then gauge your long-windedness to

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.690
help you set that goal. And, then commit
to practicing the tips in this lesson

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:12.729
and the remaining six lessons. So your
first step is get to the bottom of your

00:05:12.729 --> 00:05:17.080
unspoken reasons for long-windedness. That will really catapult you through this

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.180
course and help you apply these tips
with much more effectiveness. Looking

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:26.830
forward, we'll work on how to form a
concise overall message, concise

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.180
sentences, we'll work on organizing your
thoughts, effective pauses, avoiding

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:37.780
fillers, and confident nonverbals. If you
haven't yet subscribed to this channel,

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:42.130
Communication Coach, I encourage you to do so. And I look forward to seeing you

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.000
in the next lesson.

